# WebGL with Many Traces

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:plotly_ex, "~> 0.3"},
  {:kino, "~> 0.18"}
])
```

## Section

WebGL also handles many separate traces efficiently.
Here 10 traces of 1,000 points each — SVG would create 10,000 DOM elements;
WebGL renders all in a single GPU pass.

```elixir
alias Plotly.{Figure, Scattergl}

n_traces = 10
n_per_trace = 1_000

fig =
  Enum.reduce(1..n_traces, Figure.new(), fn i, fig ->
    x = for _ <- 1..n_per_trace, do: :rand.uniform()
    y = for _ <- 1..n_per_trace, do: :rand.uniform()

    Figure.add_trace(fig, Scattergl.new(
      x: x,
      y: y,
      mode: "markers",
      name: "Trace #{i}",
      marker: %{size: 3}
    ))
  end)

fig
|> Figure.update_layout(title: "WebGL with 10 Traces × 1,000 Points")
|> Plotly.show()
```
